' PlaybackStore — stream list for a movie/episode and its resolution to a
' playable URL, against an add-on server and the dedicated streaming server.
'
' Streams(...) speaks the Stremio add-on protocol: GET
' {addonAddress}/stream/{type}/{videoId}.json returns the streams (direct URLs
' or torrent infoHashes) for that video.
'
' A torrent stream resolves to a streaming-server URL built exactly the way the
' official Stremio client builds it — {server}/{infoHash}/{fileIdx|-1} — so a
' torrent with no index of its own needs no round trip at all: -1 tells the
' server to pick, and the file list it picks from is on the server already. The
' client's only additions are the trailing /hls.m3u8, because the service
' transcodes to Roku-native H.264/AAC and a Roku cannot play the container a
' torrent actually holds, and the `f=`/`tr=` query parameters the official
' client sends. The per-quality playlists (stream-q-<height>.m3u8) are deferred
' until we need the served bitrate control they cost in phone-server CPU.
'
' Resolution also carries the stream's container (StreamFormat), because Roku
' does not reliably sniff one and defaults to MP4: an MKV or an HLS manifest
' handed to the Video node with no format set buffers and then errors.
'
' The store is stateless like CatalogStore/EpisodesStore — every call passes
' the addresses in, nothing is persisted here. Resume positions belong to
' LibraryStore.

' The playlist probe asks a liveness question, not for a download, so it gets a
' timeout of its own rather than Transport's 15s default. The wait loop's whole
' bound is attempts x (this + the interval between them), which is only a sensible
' ceiling while a request cannot eat the default each time.
'
' Raised from 4000 to 8000 against the streaming server's own log, which is what
' settled the last unexplained timeout in this path. The server logged the engine
' being created, then our probe arriving 4.4s later, then NOTHING until a second
' probe 4s after that — roughly 8.4s from engine creation to a served playlist.
' The engine existed the whole time; it had no torrent metadata yet, and a
' request for a playlist is HELD OPEN by the server while it gathers some rather
' than refused. So a 4s deadline was not detecting a slow answer, it was
' cancelling a request the server was still legitimately working on, and the
' recovery it bought was a second identical request plus another 4s of waiting.
'
' 8000 is the ceiling that lets the first attempt actually wait out a cold
' engine. It is paid for by cutting the attempt count from six to four
' (StreamResolveTask), because the bound is attempts x (this + interval) and
' 6 x (4000 + 4000) and 4 x (8000 + 4000) are both 44s — the same worst case
' spent on one attempt that can succeed instead of two that cannot.
const PROBE_TIMEOUT_MS = 8000

' The warm-up's two timeouts, split because the two requests it makes are not
' remotely alike and one shared number was letting the wrong one fail.
'
' The HEAD asks the server to CREATE the engine, and the server's log puts that
' at 30-65ms from request to "Engine created". It is not a request that waits;
' it is a request that submits work. 4000ms is roughly a hundred times what it
' has ever taken, which is the point — a budget that large on a 40ms request is
' not headroom, it is the number that gets reused for the request below.
'
' The ranged GET is the one that actually waits. It blocks until the engine has
' torrent metadata, which for a DHT-only stream is the whole cost of a cold start
' — measured across four plays at 1.78s, 3.03s, 4.71s, and one that outlived an
' 8s cap entirely. That last one is what this number is for: the read is SUPPOSED
' to block, and a cap that expires before the engine is ready does not fail the
' stream (the probe behind it picks the wait up) but it throws away the one
' request that was doing the waiting productively, and the player starts several
' seconds later than it needed to.
'
' Together they cost 14s, less than the 16s they cost when both were 8000, so
' splitting them bought the readiness wait 2s of real headroom by taking 4s off a
' request that never used it.
const ENGINE_HEAD_TIMEOUT_MS = 4000

' The readiness read's own budget, and the only number in this file that is
' genuinely allowed to be large — it is bounded by DHT, not by us. Kept well
' under the point where it stops being a wait and starts being a hang on a server
' that will never answer, because the probe loop behind it is what decides
' whether the stream is servable and this must not become the thing that decides.
const ENGINE_READY_TIMEOUT_MS = 10000

' Torrentio permits a 30-second debrid availability check before returning its
' stream list. Leave room for the add-on's own lookup and network overhead;
' the ordinary 15-second metadata deadline can cancel a valid source response.
' This remains bounded, and a timeout is not retried by Transport.
const STREAM_LIST_TIMEOUT_MS = 45000

class PlaybackStore
    transport as object

    sub new(http as object)
        m.transport = http
    end sub

    ' GET {address}/stream/{type}/{videoId}.json — the stream candidates for a
    ' movie or episode. The response must carry a streams array.
    function Streams(addonAddress as string, metaType as string, videoId as string) as object
        result = { ok: false, streams: invalid, error: "" }
        if addonAddress = ""
            result.error = "no addon address"
            return result
        end if
        if metaType = "" or videoId = ""
            result.error = "type or video id missing"
            return result
        end if

        ' IDs are opaque provider identifiers, not URL path fragments. Escape
        ' each segment so episode IDs containing /, ?, #, or : reach the add-on
        ' intact after its normal URL decoding, without altering its config query.
        typeSegment = m.transport.EncodeQueryValue(metaType)
        idSegment = m.transport.EncodeQueryValue(videoId)
        url = m.transport.EndpointUrl(addonAddress, "/stream/" + typeSegment + "/" + idSegment + ".json")
        response = m.transport.Get(url, invalid, STREAM_LIST_TIMEOUT_MS)
        if not response.ok or response.json = invalid
            result.error = response.error
            return result
        end if

        streams = response.json.streams
        if streams = invalid or Type(streams) <> "roArray"
            result.error = "stream response missing streams"
            return result
        end if

        result.streams = streams
        result.ok = true
        return result
    end function

    ' The stream's own file index, or -1 when it gave us nothing usable as one.
    '
    ' Only a real index counts, and a name is not one. This field used to be
    ' forwarded as a search needle when it held a filename, which is a different
    ' field with a different meaning — and a DIGIT sent as a needle matches
    ' whatever release name happens to contain that digit, so fileIdx 0 found the
    ' 1080p file because of the zero in "1080p" and played it without question.
    ' The official client types this field as an integer and treats anything else
    ' as absent, and so does this: no usable index means -1, which is the
    ' server's own "you choose" answer.
    '
    ' "Is this a number" is settled by trying to make it one rather than by
    ' asking Type(). That is deliberate. Type() names the object types the same
    ' way everywhere — roArray, roAssociativeArray — but names the primitives by
    ' their own type instead of the ro-prefixed interface, so under the test
    ' interpreter a string is "String", not "roString". A Type()-based string
    ' check therefore reads FALSE in the suite and TRUE on the device: the suite
    ' would certify a branch the device never takes, and say nothing about the
    ' one it does. Coercion is the one question both answer the same way.
    function DirectFileIdx(stream as object) as integer
        if stream = invalid then return -1
        fileIdx = stream.fileIdx
        if fileIdx = invalid then return -1
        if Type(fileIdx) = "roArray" or Type(fileIdx) = "roAssociativeArray" then return -1
        try
            index = Int(fileIdx)
        catch notAnumber
            ' Not a number at all, so not an index.
            return -1
        end try
        if index < 0 then return -1
        ' A fraction coerces to a whole number, and no file list has a position
        ' 1.5, so the value has to survive the round trip unchanged.
        if index <> fileIdx then return -1
        return index
    end function

    ' A value as trimmed text, or "" when it is not text. A number throws on
    ' Trim and that throw is the answer, so a bare number can never be mistaken
    ' for a name here. Same portability reason as DirectFileIdx: a Type() check
    ' for "roString" would read false in the suite and true on the device.
    private function AsText(value as dynamic) as string
        if value = invalid then return ""
        try
            return value.Trim()
        catch notText
            return ""
        end try
    end function

    ' The name fragments for a series episode, as the streaming server's protocol
    ' spells them. This lives beside the request that sends them because the
    ' spelling IS the protocol: the server substring-matches these literals
    ' against release names, so a change here is a change to what the server is
    ' being asked, not a change to how the app talks to itself.
    '
    ' Sent as repeated `f=` parameters, which is how the official client passes
    ' fileMustInclude. The server ANDs the list, so these are two halves of one
    ' instruction, never a menu.
    '
    ' An empty list when there is nothing to ask for: a movie has no season or
    ' episode, and neither does season 0. Specials are excluded deliberately —
    ' "season: 0" does not narrow the match to the right file, it narrows it to
    ' the wrong one, and the server's own largest-video fallback is the better
    ' answer than a confident miss.
    function EpisodeFilters(season as integer, episode as integer) as object
        if season <= 0 or episode <= 0 then return []
        return ["episode: " + episode.ToStr(), "season: " + season.ToStr()]
    end function

    ' The stream's tracker list, or an empty array.
    '
    ' These are the engine's bootstrapping sources, and the official client passes
    ' every one of them straight through as `tr=` parameters. Dropping them is
    ' what made a cold engine expensive: with nothing to try, metadata has to
    ' arrive over DHT alone, which is where the long first-play stall came from.
    '
    ' The add-on protocol calls the field `sources` and the streaming-server one
    ' calls it `announce`; they are the same list and add-ons in the wild send
    ' both, so both are read, sources first. Anything that is not a list of text
    ' is dropped rather than trusted — a non-text entry would otherwise throw
    ' inside ToStr and take the whole URL down with it.
    function StreamTrackers(stream as object) as object
        if stream = invalid then return []
        sources = stream.sources
        if Type(sources) <> "roArray" then sources = stream.announce
        if Type(sources) <> "roArray" then return []
        trackers = []
        for each entry in sources
            text = m.AsText(entry)
            if text <> "" then trackers.Push(text)
        end for
        return trackers
    end function

    ' The streaming URL for a torrent, in the shape the official Stremio client
    ' builds: {server}/{infoHash}/{fileIdx|-1}, carrying the file filters and
    ' the stream's trackers as query parameters.
    '
    ' The path segment IS the file-selection protocol. A number is that index;
    ' -1 is the server's own "pick the largest video" answer, which is why a
    ' stream that arrived without an index still needs nothing asked of it. The
    ' one addition to the official shape is the trailing /hls.m3u8: the service
    ' transcodes to H.264/AAC because a Roku cannot play the container a torrent
    ' actually holds, and the Roku player wants the master playlist, so the
    ' suffix belongs on the request rather than being a separate route the
    ' official client simply has no reason to use.
    function PlaybackUrl(serverAddress as string, infoHash as string, fileIdx as integer, filters = [] as object, trackers = [] as object) as string
        return m.TorrentUrl(serverAddress, infoHash, fileIdx, filters, trackers, "/hls.m3u8")
    end function

    ' The bare torrent route — the official client shape with no HLS suffix —
    ' which is where the engine is asked into existence.
    '
    ' Built from the same stream ResolvePlayback resolved, so the two URLs cannot
    ' disagree about which file is being warmed or which trackers the engine will
    ' be handed. Takes the stream rather than the parts because the caller holds
    ' the stream, and re-deriving it here is the only way the warm-up stays in
    ' step with the URL that follows it.
    function EngineUrl(serverAddress as string, stream as object, season = 0 as integer, episode = 0 as integer) as string
        infoHash = m.StreamInfoHash(stream)
        if infoHash = "" or serverAddress = "" then return ""
        return m.TorrentUrl(serverAddress, infoHash, m.DirectFileIdx(stream), m.EpisodeFilters(season, episode), m.StreamTrackers(stream), "")
    end function

    ' The one place that knows the shape of a streaming-server torrent request:
    ' {server}/{infoHash}/{fileIdx|-1}{suffix}, carrying f= and tr= as query
    ' parameters.
    '
    ' The suffix is a parameter instead of a boolean because the only two callers
    ' differ by exactly one path segment, and a flag here would be read at the
    ' call site as an unanswerable "which one is this" — the /hls.m3u8 literal
    ' already answers it where the call is made.
    function TorrentUrl(serverAddress as string, infoHash as string, fileIdx as integer, filters as object, trackers as object, suffix as string) as string
        url = serverAddress + "/" + infoHash + "/" + fileIdx.ToStr() + suffix
        query = m.QueryString(filters, trackers)
        if query <> "" then url = url + "?" + query
        return url
    end function

    ' The query string for a torrent request: repeated f= for the file filters,
    ' then repeated tr= for the trackers.
    '
    ' REPEATED keys, not one comma-joined value. The server collects every
    ' occurrence of a key, so f=a&f=b is two constraints it ANDs together — the
    ' whole point of sending a season and an episode — while "f=a,b" would be a
    ' single constraint looking for that literal comma-and-b, which matches
    ' nothing and fails silently into the largest-video fallback.
    '
    ' Values are percent-encoded because both carry characters a query cannot
    ' hold raw: the filters contain a space and a colon, and a tracker is a URL
    ' with a scheme and a port in it.
    function QueryString(filters as object, trackers as object) as string
        parts = []
        for each filter in m.TextList(filters)
            parts.Push("f=" + m.transport.EncodeQueryValue(filter))
        end for
        for each tracker in m.TextList(trackers)
            parts.Push("tr=" + m.transport.EncodeQueryValue(tracker))
        end for
        query = ""
        for each part in parts
            if query <> "" then query = query + "&"
            query = query + part
        end for
        return query
    end function

    ' The text entries of a list, with anything that is not text dropped. Used on
    ' both query inputs, where one bad entry from an add-on must not cost the
    ' whole URL: an invalid or numeric entry throws inside ToStr, and an
    ' exception escaping here would take playback down over a malformed tracker.
    private function TextList(values as object) as object
        out = []
        if Type(values) <> "roArray" then return out
        for each value in values
            text = m.AsText(value)
            if text <> "" then out.Push(text)
        end for
        return out
    end function

    ' One-call resolution for a play button: a direct-URL stream plays as is, a
    ' torrent stream becomes a streaming-server playlist URL. Returns
    ' { ok, url, error }.
    '
    ' Nothing is asked of the server to resolve a stream. The file index is
    ' either in the stream or it is -1, and the file list that -1 is chosen from
    ' is on the server regardless, so the URL is fully determined by what is
    ' already in hand and the engine comes up when the playlist is first
    ' requested.
    '
    ' The alternative was a POST to the streaming server's create endpoint to
    ' have it pick a file, and that bought exactly one thing — a chosen index —
    ' at the price of a cold-engine park on the render thread before the player
    ' was told what to play at all: metadata over DHT and trackers, tens of
    ' seconds, with a timeout long enough to notice. That wait now happens
    ' behind the player's own buffering, where it is invisible, and the pass-
    ' through of `tr=` gives the engine the sources it was missing anyway.
    function ResolvePlayback(serverAddress as string, stream as object, season = 0 as integer, episode = 0 as integer) as object
        result = { ok: false, url: "", error: "", streamFormat: "", mode: "", headers: [] }
        if stream = invalid
            result.error = "no stream"
            return result
        end if

        url = m.AsText(stream.url)
        if url <> ""
            if LCase(url).Left(8) <> "https://" and LCase(url).Left(7) <> "http://"
                result.error = "unsupported stream URL scheme"
                return result
            end if
            result.ok = true
            result.url = url
            result.streamFormat = m.StreamFormat(stream, result.url)
            result.mode = "direct"
            result.headers = m.DirectHeaders(stream)
            return result
        end if

        infoHash = m.StreamInfoHash(stream)
        if infoHash = ""
            result.error = "stream has no infoHash"
            return result
        end if
        if serverAddress = ""
            result.error = "no streaming server address"
            return result
        end if

        result.url = m.PlaybackUrl(serverAddress, infoHash, m.DirectFileIdx(stream), m.EpisodeFilters(season, episode), m.StreamTrackers(stream))
        result.streamFormat = m.StreamFormat(stream, result.url)
        result.mode = "server"
        result.ok = true
        return result
    end function

    ' Stremio request headers belong to this media item, never to the shared
    ' app HTTP agent. Values are used only for playback and are not logged.
    ' Reject control characters/header injection and transport-owned headers.
    function DirectHeaders(stream as object) as object
        headers = []
        if stream = invalid or Type(stream.behaviorHints) <> "roAssociativeArray" then return headers
        proxyHeaders = stream.behaviorHints.proxyHeaders
        if Type(proxyHeaders) <> "roAssociativeArray" then return headers
        request = proxyHeaders.request
        if Type(request) <> "roAssociativeArray" then return headers
        for each name in request
            value = m.AsText(request[name])
            cleanName = m.AsText(name)
            lower = LCase(cleanName)
            if not m.HeaderNameValid(cleanName) then continue for
            if lower = "host" or lower = "content-length" or lower = "connection" or lower = "range" then continue for
            if not m.HeaderValueValid(value) then continue for
            headers.Push(cleanName + ":" + value)
        end for
        return headers
    end function

    private function HeaderNameValid(name as string) as boolean
        if name = "" then return false
        allowed = "abcdefghijklmnopqrstuvwxyz0123456789!#$%&'*+-.^_`|~"
        lower = LCase(name)
        for index = 0 to lower.Len() - 1
            if allowed.InStr(lower.Mid(index, 1)) < 0 then return false
        end for
        return true
    end function

    private function HeaderValueValid(value as string) as boolean
        if value = "" then return false
        for index = 0 to value.Len() - 1
            code = Asc(value.Mid(index, 1))
            if code < 32 or code = 127 then return false
        end for
        return true
    end function

    function StreamInfoHash(stream as object) as string
        if stream <> invalid and stream.infoHash <> invalid then return stream.infoHash.ToStr()
        return ""
    end function

    ' Whether this stream is a torrent the streaming server has to serve, as
    ' opposed to a direct URL the player opens as-is.
    '
    ' Mirrors the precedence ResolvePlayback applies, deliberately, so the two can
    ' never disagree about the same stream: if a stream somehow carries both a
    ' playable URL and a hash, the URL wins there and the stream is not a torrent
    ' here.
    function IsTorrent(stream as object) as boolean
        if stream = invalid then return false
        url = m.AsText(stream.url)
        if url <> "" then return false
        return m.StreamInfoHash(stream) <> ""
    end function

    ' The container the Video node must be told to play, or "" when nothing can be
    ' inferred. Roku defaults streamFormat to H.264/AAC in an MP4 container, so a
    ' format left unset is not "let the platform sniff it" — it is actively wrong
    ' for an MKV or an HLS manifest, and the symptom is a buffer that ends in an
    ' immediate error (errorCode -5, "media format unknown or unsupported").
    '
    ' Which hint wins is deliberate. A manifest URL (m3u8/mpd) IS the transport,
    ' not a media file, so it is read from the URL and never overridden: a torrent
    ' resolves to our /hls.m3u8 transcode, and declaring the source file's
    ' container there would break the very transcoding that makes it playable.
    ' A recognizable delivery URL wins even when an add-on names the original
    ' pre-conversion file. For opaque URLs, each filename candidate is consulted
    ' independently, in the
    ' protocol's order of trust — behaviorHints.filename, then title, then
    ' description — and only believed when it actually names a known container:
    ' an extensionless hint must not shadow a title that ends in a real
    ' extension, which is a real add-on shape (a slug in the hint, the release
    ' name with its extension in the title). The description is read because
    ' some add-ons keep the release name there instead of in the title — the
    ' same field the stream card renders, on the principle that the detector
    ' should hear everything the user is shown. `name` is never a candidate: it
    ' holds the provider/quality label, and a label like "1080p" is not a
    ' filename. The URL is the last resort, because a direct link can hide the
    ' container entirely (a presigned URL keeps the filename in its query, not
    ' in its path).
    function StreamFormat(stream as object, url as string) as string
        urlFormat = m.FormatFromUrl(url)
        if urlFormat <> "" then return urlFormat
        if stream <> invalid
            hint = m.FormatFromText(m.AddonFilename(stream))
            if hint <> "" then return hint
            hint = m.FormatFromText(m.AddonTitle(stream))
            if hint <> "" then return hint
            hint = m.FormatFromText(m.AddonDescription(stream))
            if hint <> "" then return hint
        end if
        return urlFormat
    end function

    ' The add-on's dedicated filename slot, or "". Read as a candidate, not the
    ' answer: some add-ons put a non-filename there, and the caller decides.
    private function AddonFilename(stream as object) as string
        hints = stream.behaviorHints
        if hints = invalid or Type(hints) <> "roAssociativeArray" then return ""
        return m.AsText(hints.filename)
    end function

    ' The add-on's title, or "". Scanned whole, not down to its first line: the
    ' extension rule already looks for the LAST bounded extension anywhere in the
    ' string, and a line feed is a boundary, so the size/seeders lines that follow
    ' a release name cannot hide its container.
    private function AddonTitle(stream as object) as string
        return m.AsText(stream.title)
    end function

    ' The add-on's description, or "". Several add-ons that leave title empty put
    ' the release name, size and quality here instead (the stream card renders it
    ' too), so an empty title is not a dead end.
    private function AddonDescription(stream as object) as string
        return m.AsText(stream.description)
    end function

    ' The container a URL names: the path first, the query only as a fallback. The
    ' query matters because a presigned link puts the real filename in
    ' response-content-disposition ("...filename=%22movie.mkv%22") while the path
    ' is an opaque object id with no extension at all.
    private function FormatFromUrl(url as string) as string
        cleanUrl = LCase(url.Trim())
        fragmentIndex = cleanUrl.InStr("#")
        if fragmentIndex >= 0 then cleanUrl = cleanUrl.Left(fragmentIndex)
        queryIndex = cleanUrl.InStr("?")
        if queryIndex >= 0
            path = cleanUrl.Left(queryIndex)
            query = cleanUrl.Mid(queryIndex + 1)
        else
            path = cleanUrl
            query = ""
        end if
        format = m.FormatFromText(path)
        if format <> "" then return format
        if query = "" then return ""
        ' Signed tokens and tracking parameters are not filenames. An arbitrary
        ' token ending in .mpd must not turn an MKV into a DASH request.
        for each part in query.Split("&")
            equalIndex = part.InStr("=")
            if equalIndex < 0 then continue for
            key = part.Left(equalIndex)
            if key <> "filename" and key <> "file" and key <> "name" and key <> "response-content-disposition" then continue for
            value = part.Mid(equalIndex + 1).Replace("%2e", ".")
            format = m.FormatFromText(value)
            if format <> "" then return format
        end for
        return ""
    end function

    ' The container named by the LAST file extension in `text`, or "". The last
    ' one, because a release name carries earlier dots ("Show.2026.mkv") and the
    ' file's extension is the final one. An extension only counts when it ends the
    ' string or meets a character that cannot be part of a filename, which is what
    ' lets a percent-encoded query filename (".mkv%22") still be found while
    ' refusing to read a container out of the middle of a word.
    private function FormatFromText(text as string) as string
        if text = "" then return ""
        lower = LCase(text)
        extensions = [".m3u8", ".m3u", ".mpd", ".mkv", ".mp4", ".m4v", ".mov"]
        formats = ["hls", "hls", "dash", "mkv", "mp4", "mp4", "mp4"]
        bestIndex = -1
        bestFormat = ""
        for i = 0 to extensions.Count() - 1
            index = m.LastBoundedIndex(lower, extensions[i])
            if index > bestIndex
                bestIndex = index
                bestFormat = formats[i]
            end if
        end for
        return bestFormat
    end function

    ' The index of the final occurrence of `ext` in `text` that ends at a
    ' boundary, or -1. Scanned by shrinking the string rather than with InStr's
    ' optional start argument, which this codebase has never put on a device.
    private function LastBoundedIndex(text as string, ext as string) as integer
        found = -1
        offset = 0
        index = text.InStr(ext)
        while index >= 0
            after = index + ext.Len()
            if after >= text.Len() or m.IsExtensionBoundary(text.Mid(after, 1))
                found = offset + index
            end if
            offset = offset + index + 1
            text = text.Mid(index + 1)
            index = text.InStr(ext)
        end while
        return found
    end function

    ' Whether a character ends an extension — anything that cannot be part of a
    ' filename (letter, digit or dot). Callers pass lowercased text.
    private function IsExtensionBoundary(ch as string) as boolean
        if ch = "" then return true
        filenameChars = "abcdefghijklmnopqrstuvwxyz0123456789."
        return filenameChars.InStr(ch) < 0
    end function

    ' HEAD the bare torrent route so the streaming server has created the engine
    ' before the playlist is ever asked for.
    '
    ' Stremio's own client opens every torrent this way, and the server log shows
    ' the engine appearing on the back of that request. Asking for the playlist
    ' first is what left the probe timing out six times over: that was not a slow
    ' engine being raced, it was a server that had never been asked to make one.
    '
    ' Best effort, and never fatal. The probe loop behind this is what decides
    ' whether the stream is servable and it runs either way, so a warm-up that
    ' fails costs the optimisation and nothing else — which is why the caller can
    ' keep it in a try of its own.
    '
    ' The status comes back for the reason the probe's does: to be able to say
    ' afterwards whether the request reached the server at all. A 2xx here is not
    ' logged by the transport trace, so this record is the only trace of it.
    '
    ' Then the forced-metadata read, which is the request that actually makes the
    ' engine able to answer. The HEAD above only asks the server to CREATE the
    ' engine, and it returns in milliseconds — so "HEAD 200" was never evidence
    ' the engine could serve a playlist, and the probe behind this was left
    ' discovering the difference the slow way, one 4s timeout at a time.
    '
    ' Stremio's client does not poll for readiness at all. It asks for the first
    ' 64KB of the file and lets THAT block until the engine has metadata, and the
    ' server's log for a working device shows exactly that request sitting between
    ' the HEAD and the /hls.m3u8. So this mirrors the client's own sequence rather
    ' than inventing a readiness protocol: one ranged GET converts "the engine
    ' exists" into "the engine can serve", and the probe behind it then hits a
    ' ready engine on attempt 1.
    '
    ' The Range header is what keeps this from being the mistake the HEAD comment
    ' above warns about. An unbounded GET on this route is a request to buffer an
    ' entire feature into a string; 64KB is enough to prove the engine can serve
    ' bytes and small enough to be free. The body is discarded — this is a
    ' readiness signal, not data, and the player fetches its own segments
    ' afterwards.
    '
    ' Recorded separately rather than folded into `ok`, because whether the engine
    ' was CREATED and whether it went READY are different questions and only the
    ' first is answered by the HEAD. A cold engine and an unreachable server are
    ' indistinguishable without the second, which is the whole difference between
    ' "wait longer" and "this stream is not coming".
    function WarmEngine(url as string) as object
        result = { ok: false, status: 0, error: "", range: invalid }
        if url = "" then
            result.error = "no engine url"
            return result
        end if
        raw = m.transport.Head(url, ENGINE_HEAD_TIMEOUT_MS)
        if raw = invalid
            result.error = "no transport"
            return result
        end if
        result.ok = raw.ok
        result.status = raw.status
        if raw.error <> invalid then result.error = raw.error

        ' Not guarded on the HEAD's own success. A refused or timed-out HEAD still
        ' leaves the server building the engine, and this request is the one that
        ' finds out whether it did — so skipping it on a failed HEAD would throw
        ' away the only remaining evidence exactly when there is least of it.
        ranged = m.transport.GetRaw(url, ENGINE_READY_TIMEOUT_MS, { "Range": "bytes=0-65535" })
        if ranged <> invalid then
            result.range = { ok: ranged.ok, status: ranged.status, error: ranged.error }
        end if
        return result
    end function

    ' One plain GET of the playlist URL, with the body left as text.
    '
    ' This is the probe the player is NOT asked to make. Whatever the server says
    ' here is what the Video node is about to be told, a moment earlier and on a
    ' thread whose patience is ours rather than the node's — and the node's is
    ' much the shorter of the two.
    '
    ' No retrying and no sleeping here: the store is asked a question and answers
    ' it. How many times to keep asking, and how long to pause between asks, is a
    ' property of the thread doing the asking, so it belongs to the resolve task.
    ' That split is not tidiness, it is testability — a Wait() inside store code
    ' ABORTS the brs interpreter's run and Ticks() does not exist in it, so a wait
    ' loop written here would be neither exercised by the suite nor timed.
    function ProbePlaylist(url as string) as object
        probe = { ok: false, status: 0, body: "", error: "" }
        raw = m.transport.GetRaw(url, PROBE_TIMEOUT_MS)
        if raw = invalid
            probe.error = "no transport"
            return probe
        end if
        probe.ok = raw.ok
        probe.status = raw.status
        probe.body = m.BodyHead(raw.body)
        if raw.error <> invalid then probe.error = raw.error
        return probe
    end function

    ' The opening of a response body, kept for the diagnostic that says whether
    ' the server handed back a manifest at all. Long enough to identify one, short
    ' enough that a segment list cannot fill a label or a log file.
    private function BodyHead(body as dynamic) as string
        if body = invalid then return ""
        text = body.ToStr()
        if Len(text) <= 400 then return text
        return text.Left(400)
    end function

    ' Liveness check for the streaming server: GET /heartbeat → { success: true }.
    function Heartbeat(serverAddress as string) as object
        result = { ok: false, alive: false, error: "" }
        if serverAddress = ""
            result.error = "no streaming server address"
            return result
        end if

        response = m.transport.Get(serverAddress + "/heartbeat")
        if response.ok and response.json <> invalid and response.json.success = true
            result.ok = true
            result.alive = true
        else
            if response.error <> "" then
                result.error = response.error
            else
                result.error = "unexpected heartbeat response"
            end if
        end if
        return result
    end function
end class
